{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by the screenwriter Aaron sorkin. the film is based on the book 'the accidental billionaire' by ben Mezrich, which tells the story of the founding of the social networking site facebook and the legal battles that followed.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, a harvard college student who creates a website called facemash, a site that allows users to compare the photos of two students and vote on which one is more attractive. the site becomes popular, but also attracts the ire of the harvard administration, which leads to mark's site being shut down.\n\nmark then creates a new site called thefacebook, which is initially intended as a networking site for harvard students. however, the site quickly gains popularity and spreads to other colleges and eventually becomes a global phenomenon.\n\nthe film also stars andrew garfield as edward saverin, mark's best friend and business partner; justin timberlake as sean parker, a brilliant programmer who helps mark launch thefacebook; and joshua jackson as trey parker, a fellow harvard student who helps mark with the early development of the site.\n\nthe film explores the complex and often tumultuous relationship between mark and his best friend edward, who becomes increasingly disenchanted with mark's treatment of him as thefacebook grows in popularity. the film also examines the role of sean parker, who is portrayed as a charismatic and ruthless businessman who helps mark launch the site but also becomes embroiled in a bitter dispute with him over ownership and control.\n\none of the standout aspects of the social network is its sharp and witty dialogue, which is courtesy of aaron sorkin's clever script. the film's use of rapid-fire dialogue and clever banter adds to its sense of energy and urgency, and helps to bring the characters to life.\n\nthe film also features a number of memorable performances, including jesse eisenberg's nuanced portrayal of mark zuckerberg. eisenberg brings a depth and complexity to the character, capturing both his genius and his flaws. andrew garfield is also excellent as edward saverin, bringing a sense of vulnerability and anger to the role.\n\nthe film's direction by david fincher is also noteworthy. fincher brings a sense of cool and detachment to the film, which helps to capture the sense of detachment and disconnection that mark feels as thefacebook grows in popularity. the film's use of long takes and clever camera work adds to its sense of style and sophistication.\n\noverall, the social network is a gripping and thought-provoking film that explores the complex and often tumultuous world of the tech industry. with its sharp dialogue, memorable performances, and stylish direction, the film is a must-see for anyone interested in the story of facebook and the people who helped create it.\n\nrating: 9/10", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.045232724}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0016868184}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -7.6293654e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.18040486}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00012385078}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.32515162}, {"token": "201", "logprob": -2.9086643e-05}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -3.933899e-06}, {"token": " american", "logprob": -0.3342006}, {"token": " bi", "logprob": -0.0031711787}, {"token": "ographical", "logprob": -0.00043990472}, {"token": " drama", "logprob": -0.011117537}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.00057299406}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -0.18416922}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -0.0021900972}, {"token": " david", "logprob": -0.009290322}, {"token": " fin", "logprob": -0.00041380903}, {"token": "cher", "logprob": -2.682173e-05}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.2561861}, {"token": " written", "logprob": -0.0026351034}, {"token": " b